    {
      "id": "phrase-alimentar-al-troll",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "alimentar al troll",
      "questionEn": "What does alimentar al troll mean in an online discussion?",
      "answerEn": "It means to engage with a provocateur in a way that rewards or encourages more provocation.",
      "noteEn": "Alimentar literally means “to feed,” but this internet expression uses feeding as a metaphor for giving a troll the attention they seek. Al combines a and el before the person being fed. The phrase often appears in advice such as no alimentes al troll.",
      "example": { "es": "Ignora el mensaje; no alimentes al troll.", "en": "Ignore the message; do not feed the troll." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "internet", "idiom", "communication", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-tanto-que-result",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "verbo + tanto que + resultado",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone does an action so much that a particular result follows?",
      "answerEn": "Use the action verb, then tanto, then que before the result.",
      "noteEn": "The plus signs in the prompt mean “followed by.” The action verb changes for the person and time, while tanto stays unchanged when it describes the degree of an action. Que introduces the consequence. This differs from tan, which normally comes before a describing word such as difícil.",
      "example": { "es": "Trabajó tanto que se quedó dormido.", "en": "He worked so much that he fell asleep." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "degree", "result", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-a-mi-tambien",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "a mí también",
      "questionEn": "How do you say “me too” when the same statement applies to you?",
      "answerEn": "Use a mí también.",
      "noteEn": "Mí is the stressed form of “me” used after a, and it carries a written accent. The complete expression can stand alone as a reply or be followed by a repeated verb. The a has no separate English word in the natural reply “me too.”",
      "example": { "es": "—Me gusta el jazz. —A mí también.", "en": "—I like jazz. —Me too." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "agreement", "conversation", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-actuarial-mortality-tables",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "tablas de mortalidad · actuaría",
      "answerEn": "Mortality tables summarize death rates by age or group; actuaries use them to estimate life expectancy and financial risk.",
      "noteEn": "The tables describe probabilities across populations, not exact death dates for particular people. Turning them into precise predictions about named people's deaths exaggerates what the statistics can do and makes the calculation deliberately morbid.",
      "tags": ["concept", "actuarial science", "statistics", "mortality", "xkcd joke"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-nunca-jamas",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "nunca jamás",
      "questionEn": "How do you strengthen “never” to mean “never ever”?",
      "answerEn": "Use nunca jamás for an emphatic “never ever.”",
      "noteEn": "Nunca and jamás can each express “never.” Putting them together intensifies the refusal or promise; Spanish negative words reinforce one another rather than canceling each other. The expression can appear before or after the verb according to the ordinary rules for Spanish negatives.",
      "example": { "es": "Nunca jamás volveré allí.", "en": "I will never ever go back there." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "negation", "emphasis", "A2"]
    }
  ],
